      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Develop the primary exact algorithm: replace the O(S·N²) snake traversal with a row-streaming separable S×S window computation, using contiguous S-row storage plus per-column rolling sums and a horizontal rolling sum. Target O(N²) work, O(S·N) matrix storage, 64-bit intermediates, and one exact maximum for every valid input.",
        "B": "Focus on sequential throughput around the primary algorithm: benchmark-safe fast integer input, contiguous/cache-friendly layout, minimized allocation and branch overhead, and ARM64-friendly scalar/vector code where it demonstrably helps. Preserve parsing of arbitrary whitespace, signs, EOF, and exact textual output; do not rely on input values or test-specific shortcuts.",
        "C": "Research a separately measurable four-core strategy for the O(N²) formulation. Explore low-overhead row/column striping or a staged box-filter pipeline with deterministic local maxima and reduction; account for serial input, synchronization, memory bandwidth, and S-dependent overhead. Retain a strong sequential fallback because parallelism may lose on the 0.134 s baseline regime.",
        "G": "Integrate only compatible, measured improvements from A/B/C. Use the streaming O(N²) design as the likely base, select parallel code only when end-to-end measurements win, and validate broad valid cases including S=1, S=N-1, negative values, ties, and varied whitespace. Keep the final program general, exact, and free of test-specialization.",
        "analysis": "The supplied baseline is valid but performs O(S·N²) update work and uses fragmented full-matrix storage; its measured mean is 0.13359 s on a 4-core ARM64 target. The dominant opportunity is an asymptotically better rolling box sum, with parsing and memory locality likely important afterward. Parallel gains are uncertain because input and memory traffic can dominate, so integration must be evidence-driven rather than additive."
